  "verdict": "Withdraw. Both gates together move the decomposed wrong share from 24.8% to 24.3% while recall falls from 0.869 to 0.686 -- 0.5 points of precision for 18 of recall. No role reaches an importable standard: GOAL, the best, is 11.4% wrong; PATIENT 18.8%; AGENT 30.9%; LOCATION 31.2%. The AGENT class splits by case, and the larger half -- an agreeing nominative under a third-person verb -- is 39.8% wrong. There is no threshold at which the rule is safe to assert.",
  "what_was_done_instead": "Asserted role fillers now come only from the DCS dependency parse: 51,231 -> 2,052. Everywhere else the assertion is predicate-only, carrying predicate, frame, verb surface and verb features, all read off the verb token and none dependent on role scope, with roles_withheld and a reason. The gated candidates are retained in role_candidates.jsonl with importable=false as a verification queue: 32,025 candidates over 15,704 assertions.",
  "why_not_simply_downgrade_the_row": "mapping_confidence is a row-level field and a row carries many assertions. Downgrading the row to PROBABLE would have made the defensible predicate layer non-importable along with the undefendable roles. Splitting by what each claim rests on keeps the sound part importable and the unsound part out of rows.jsonl entirely, which is stronger than a confidence label on a mixed row.",
  "residual_risk_named": "The DCS parse is the reference here and it is not infallible: DCS's own readme claims only that each string was 'verified by one annotator'. So a disagreement is not automatically this pipeline's error. That cannot excuse a 24.3% disagreement rate, but it does mean the 2,052 parse-backed fillers inherit single-annotator quality rather than adjudicated gold, and no human has reviewed any of them."
